{*
   base-article.latte - the middle of the chain.

   It extends base.latte and is extended in turn by a template held in the
   database, which makes the demo three levels deep:

       base.latte  <-  base-article.latte  <-  "aLatteX Demo: Extends, article"

   Nothing about the middle is special to Latte - a parent is just a template
   that happens to extend another one - but it is the level where a real site
   puts the shape shared by one *kind* of page, with base.latte holding what
   every page shares.

   {include parent} is the tag worth reading here: it renders the block as the
   parent defined it and adds to it, rather than replacing it. base.latte's
   crumbs survive, with this level appended.
*}
{extends 'base.latte'}

{block bodyClass}alx-base alx-article{/block}

{block crumbs}
    {include parent} / <span class="alx-dim">article</span>
{/block}

{block content}
    <article class="alx-article-body">
        <header>
            <h1>{block heading}{$pagetitle}{/block}</h1>
            <p class="alx-dim">{block standfirst}An article laid out by base-article.latte.{/block}</p>
        </header>

        {block body}
            <p>base-article.latte defines the frame; the child fills the body.</p>
        {/block}
    </article>
{/block}

{block aside}
    <p>Article aside, from base-article.latte.</p>
    {block asideExtra}{/block}
{/block}
